-- title:
--   Weight symmetry n↦-n at a real place
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a number field, let $c,u,d_1,d_2$ be real numbers with $d_1<d_2$, and let $T$ be a finite subset of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_F)$. Write $D=\bigcup_{x\in T}\{g x : g\in \mathrm{centreCutSiegelSet}\}$, where `centreCutSiegelSet F c u d₁ d₂` consists of those $g$ whose finite part lies in `finiteIntegralGL2`, whose archimedean component at every infinite place has `localHeight` at least $c$ and `xWindowSq` at most $u^2$, and whose `archDetNorm` at every infinite place lies in $[d_1,d_2]$. Assume `CoversModCentre F` $D$: for every $g\in\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_F)$ there are $\gamma\in\mathrm{GL}_2(F)$ and $z\in\mathbb{A}_F^\times$ with $\gamma g\,z\in D$ (the images being taken under `globalPoints` and `centralScalar`). Let $\Theta$ be a Hecke eigensystem over $F$ with complex coefficients (a nonzero level ideal together with families $a,b$ indexed by the finite primes), let $w$ be a real infinite place of $F$, and let $n\in\mathbb{Z}$. Assume `ArchOccursInClassOf F` $D$ $\Theta$ $P_n$, i.e. there is an eigensystem $\Theta'$ whose $a$- and $b$-values agree with those of $\Theta$ outside a finite set of primes, together with a smooth cusp realisation $R'$ at the production pins built on $D$ (Haar measure and Borel structure on $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_F)$, full central subgroup, level subgroups $N\mapsto$ `levelOne` intersected with the kernel of `glArch`, Hecke generators `heckeGen`, and the adelic additive Haar measure conditioned on `adelicBox`) for `Θ'.toRawCentral` (the eigensystem with $b_v$ rescaled by $(\mathrm{cNorm}\,v)^{-1}$), such that `R'.toFun` is continuous and satisfies $P_n$; here $P_n(\varphi)$ is `HasArchCharacterAt₀ F w` applied to the weight-$n$ character `archWeightCharℝ n` transported along the identification of the completion at $w$ with $\mathbb{R}$. The conclusion is the same assertion with $P_{-n}$ in place of $P_n$.
--
--   This is the reflection symmetry of the $\mathrm{SO}(2)$-types occurring at a real place within a near-equivalence class of cuspidal eigensystems: conjugation by $\mathrm{diag}(1,-1)$ inverts the characters of the rotation subgroup, so weight $n$ occurs precisely when weight $-n$ does. It is used in the analysis of the $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$-module generated by a minimal type, in the lowering-operator annihilation criterion, and in the identification of Casimir eigenvalues in the Langlands–Tunnell part of the development.
